*Locate.* Neural and structural signals perform better than keywords (@tab-heldout, @fig-retrieval-js). The neural dense ranker beats BM25 on JavaScript questions held-out (+0.178 MRR, Holm p = 0.015) and Python (+0.191, Holm p = 0.0009) questions, and outperforms LSA (+0.251). Fusion of BM25, dense, and graph ranking is the best ranking strategy among all three categories (0.58, 0.69, 0.61), and beats BM25 by +0.260 on held-out JavaScript (Holm p = 0.0009). The difference from dense alone (+0.08 on held-out) is descriptive. For each of the repositories (@tab-repo), fusion beats BM25 on all held-out repositories with one exception, while the neural ranker beats BM25 on all Python repositories. The neural advantage on held-out JavaScript is less general: it is observed on three repositories with 72 out of 79 locate questions, while it is reversed in `body-parser` (6 questions) and `request` (1 question), the difference between the two having an interval which contains zero. The original CodeAI retriever (v1, 0.43) is better than plain BM25 because of the symbol-name bonus, but still falls behind both the neural ranker and fusion.

*Direct importers.* The graph ranker achieves near perfection (0.97 held-out, 1.00 on the other two groups) and beats BM25 by +0.294 (Holm p = 0.0009). Fusion decreases this gap (RRF LSA − graph = −0.100; not significant after correction) since text rankers boost non-importers. As pointed out in @sec-questions, this result confirms the correctness of the import graph.

*Change impact.* Graph-based rankings beat text-only rankings (held-out nDCG\@10: 0.74 for the graph, 0.59 for BM25, and 0.27 for dense), although the small size of the held-out dataset (37 questions) is not enough for the difference to be significant (p = 0.083). Graphs beat BM25 on four held-out repositories but lag behind on `redux-saga` (15 questions). Neural embeddings have little value here since the files depending on `utils.js` are not required to be similar to it.

